# "Decode As" entries file for the ESP32-C6 802.15.4 profile.
#
# Wireshark's Matter dissector registers itself on Bluetooth only: the
# commissioning service UUID 0xFFF6 and its BTP transport. It claims no UDP
# port, so Matter carried over Thread arrives correctly decrypted and is then
# shown as plain UDP, with the "Matter" display filter matching nothing. The
# dissector is present and works -- it is simply never reached.
#
# This is deliberate upstream, not an oversight. The dissector's author
# declined to claim a port because implementations may pick an arbitrary one
# and advertise it over mDNS, so Matter is offered through Decode As only
# (Wireshark merge request 9865). The dissector landed in Wireshark 4.2; on
# older releases this entry names a dissector that does not exist and is
# ignored, which costs nothing.
#
# 5540 is Matter's standard operational port. Pointing the dissector at it
# turns those frames into Matter messages with session identifiers, counters and
# acknowledgements. Verified against live Thread traffic: frames that read
# "56368 -> 5540 Len=42" in the protocol column became "Matter, Unicast
# Session, Counter=...", with the session and counter values filled in.
#
# The last field must be the dissector's DESCRIPTION, "Matter", not its filter
# name "matter". Measured against Wireshark 4.6.8: "matter" and "MATTER" both
# yield zero frames and, importantly, no error message of any kind -- a
# misspelling here is indistinguishable from an absent network key. There is a
# test guarding this exact string in host/tests/test_profiles.py.
#
# This needs the Thread network key installed as well, otherwise the frames
# never decrypt far enough to become UDP. See host/tools/thread_key.py.
decode_as_entry: udp.port,5540,(none),Matter
